  <static seed="37" source="Library/WHFreeman/Holt_linear_algebra/Chaps_1-4/3.3.48.pg">
    <statement><p>If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible <m>n\times n</m> matrices,
then the inverse of <m>A+B</m> is <m>A^{-1}+B^{-1}</m>.<ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0001"><li>
</li><li>
<p>True</p>
</li><li>
<p>False</p>
</li></ul></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{False}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><alert>SOLUTION:</alert> 
False. For example, let <m>A = I_n</m>, and <m>B = -I_n</m>, then <m>A+B = 0_n</m>, which is not invertible.</p>
</solution>
